  {
    "title": "Show HN: Pretty Git Tree – Your Git history as generative art",
    "description": "<p>I've spent months building something complex and (so far) invisible. I know that the number of commits, branches, merges etc. aren't proof of progress, but what I needed was a way to represent the amount of effort I'd put in that wasn't \"proximity to burnout\".<p>Pretty-git-tree takes in your git history (just the commit structure, and it stays local to your browser) and gives you a visually interesting representation of it. For my own projects I use the \"qubits\" style which winds a quantum circuit around a space-filling curve. I've included snapshots of some famous git repositories as well.<p>I didn't want to just use the standard git tree, because I felt that there were so many different ways of exploring the data - plus I wanted it to look good at the top of a weekly standup report. I've included the four styles I expect people to use, plus another 15 or so styles in the repo where I wanted to try things out (like rendering the branches as tower-blocks in a city, or kintsugi fractures). The repo itself is MIT licensed, and suggestions are welcome.</p>\n<hr />\n<p>Comments URL: <a href=\"https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50292976\">https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50292976</a></p>\n<p>Points: 2</p>\n<p># Comments: 0</p>",
    "url": "https://hmillerbakewell.github.io/pretty-git-tree/",
    "source": "Hacker News",
    "label": "Developer Projects",
    "discovered_at": "2026-10-29T17:47:49.206146+00:00"
  },
